Events are how JavaScript reacts to the user — clicks, typing, hovering, submitting forms.

### Listening to events
```js
const button = document.querySelector("#btn");
button.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("Button clicked!");
});
```
- The **event type**: `"click"`, `"input"`, `"submit"`, `"mouseover"`, etc.
- The **handler**: a function that runs when the event fires

### Common events
| Event | Fires when |
|-------|-----------|
| `click` | Element is clicked |
| `input` | A field's value changes |
| `change` | A select/checkbox changes |
| `submit` | A form is submitted |
| `keydown` | A key is pressed |

### Reading the event
```js
input.addEventListener("input", (event) => {
  console.log(event.target.value);  // current typed text
});
```
`event.target` is the element that fired the event.

### A live counter
```js
let count = 0;
const counter = document.querySelector("#count");

document.querySelector("#plus").addEventListener("click", () => {
  count += 1;
  counter.textContent = count;
});
```

### Form submit
```js
document.querySelector("form").addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();   // stop the page reloading
  const name = document.querySelector("#name").value;
  alert(`Hi, ${name}!`);
});
```
Always call `event.preventDefault()` in submit handlers unless you really want a page reload.
